Part 1.

37
00:03:03.479 --> 00:03:05.399
Introduction and Why It Matters.

38
00:03:06.649 --> 00:03:08.569
A story: the cart that forgot.

39
00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:10.812
It is the first hour of a big sale.

40
00:03:11.092 --> 00:03:16.148
A replica group behind the cart service loses contact with its peers for four minutes.

41
00:03:16.428 --> 00:03:20.780
The cart team had chosen strong consistency, so writes to that group fail.

42
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:29.017
Across the affected carts, Add to cart shows an error, and conversion for those shoppers drops to nearly zero while the network heals.

43
00:03:30.367 --> 00:03:38.687
The post-incident question is blunt: is a shopper ever better off seeing can't add to cart than seeing a cart that might need merging later?

44
00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:40.653
For a cart, almost never.

45
00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:47.717
That is the reasoning behind the Dynamo paper's requirement that the shopping cart be always writable (VERIFY:

46
00:03:47.997 --> 00:03:49.191
DeCandia et al.

47
00:03:49.471 --> 00:03:50.325
Dynamo:

48
00:03:50.605 --> 00:03:54.893
Amazon's Highly Available Key-value Store, SOSP 2007).

49
00:03:55.173 --> 00:04:01.829
It also explains the famous side-effect the paper describes: deleted items can reappear after a merge.

50
00:04:03.179 --> 00:04:06.059
Global marketplaces are full of these choices.

51
00:04:06.339 --> 00:04:12.717
Where do you give up consistency for availability (the cart), and where must you not (payment, the last unit of stock)?

52
00:04:12.997 --> 00:04:15.877
How do millions of sellers share one product page?

53
00:04:16.157 --> 00:04:19.421
How do you promise a delivery date before the order exists?

54
00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:24.693
And how do you stop one bad customer or one bad deploy from taking down everyone?

55
00:04:26.043 --> 00:04:26.726
Quote:

56
00:04:27.006 --> 00:04:28.670
The thesis of this chapter.

57
00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.409
A global marketplace is many small, owned services composed per page.

58
00:04:33.689 --> 00:04:55.875
Each service chooses its own consistency model: carts are always writable and merge correctly (L1);. offers are aggregated into a single winning offer that is maintained, not recomputed (L2);. promises are computed from where stock is, and stock placement matters more than routing (L3);. failures are contained by cells and shuffle sharding (L4).

