200
00:10:41.489 --> 00:10:42.171
Why:

201
00:10:42.451 --> 00:10:43.731
Ch 43.

202
00:10:45.081 --> 00:10:49.433
The pattern is consistency chosen per operation, not per company.

203
00:10:49.713 --> 00:10:53.276
It is the same principle as Ch 59 Q18.

204
00:10:54.626 --> 00:10:56.311
The always-writable cart.

205
00:10:56.591 --> 00:11:01.370
Requirement: add-to-cart must succeed even when some replicas are unreachable.

206
00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:08.007
That means a leaderless or multi-leader store with sloppy quorums and hinted handoff (Ch 54).

207
00:11:08.287 --> 00:11:16.223
Consequence: replicas can hold concurrent versions (siblings), detected with version vectors, and the application must merge them.

208
00:11:16.503 --> 00:11:18.381
The merge is a product decision.

209
00:11:18.661 --> 00:11:21.882
Last-writer-wins throws away a whole replica's cart.

210
00:11:22.162 --> 00:11:28.434
L1 lost 36,206 of 95,886 adds.

211
00:11:29.784 --> 00:11:33.517
Union of siblings keeps adds but brings back removed items.

212
00:11:33.797 --> 00:11:40.368
L1 resurrected 12,184 removes, the behaviour the Dynamo paper describes.

213
00:11:40.648 --> 00:11:42.141
An OR-Set tags each add.

214
00:11:42.421 --> 00:11:47.819
A remove deletes only the tags the client had seen, so adds and removes both survive.

215
00:11:48.099 --> 00:11:50.317
In L1 every cart ended up right.

216
00:11:50.597 --> 00:11:53.093
Cost of an OR-Set: tag metadata grows.

217
00:11:53.373 --> 00:11:58.173
Garbage-collect it when all replicas have seen a remove (Ch 10 CRDTs).

218
00:11:59.523 --> 00:12:02.403
Where strong consistency returns: at checkout.

219
00:12:02.683 --> 00:12:10.321
Prices, stock and promotions are re-validated against their sources of truth, and the cart is only a wish list with quantities.

220
00:12:11.671 --> 00:12:13.911
Catalog, offers and the buy box.

221
00:12:14.191 --> 00:12:15.812
One product, many offers.

222
00:12:16.092 --> 00:12:21.724
Sellers attach offers (price, shipping, condition, fulfilment method) to one catalog entry.

223
00:12:22.004 --> 00:12:27.679
Duplicate listings are merged by product identifiers and matching (Ch 21).

224
00:12:27.959 --> 00:12:34.679
The winning offer (buy box) is chosen by a score: landed price, delivery promise, seller performance, and stock.

225
00:12:34.959 --> 00:12:39.737
The real criteria are not public, so L2 uses an illustrative formula.

226
00:12:40.017 --> 00:12:44.625
It is read on every product page and changes whenever any seller reprices.

227
00:12:44.905 --> 00:12:48.575
Automated repricers can change prices many times an hour.

228
00:12:48.855 --> 00:12:49.751
Choices:

229
00:12:51.101 --> 00:12:56.349
Compute on read: simple and always current, but it costs a query per page (L2:

230
00:12:56.629 --> 00:12:58.655
29k reads per second).

231
00:12:58.935 --> 00:13:03.629
Maintain on write: upsert the winner in the same transaction as the offer change.

232
00:13:03.909 --> 00:13:08.517
Reads are about 2 times faster; writes cost ~30% more (L2).

233
00:13:08.797 --> 00:13:11.549
Precompute plus cache: fastest, but stale.

234
00:13:11.829 --> 00:13:14.559
Acceptable if checkout re-validates the price.

235
00:13:14.839 --> 00:13:15.735
The race.

236
00:13:17.085 --> 00:13:19.688
Two sellers reprice the same product at once.

237
00:13:19.968 --> 00:13:25.344
Each transaction computes the winner from a snapshot that cannot see the other's uncommitted change.

238
00:13:25.624 --> 00:13:33.283
The later commit can install the wrong winner, and it stays wrong until the next change to that product (L2's forced interleaving).

239
00:13:33.563 --> 00:13:37.445
Fixes: serialize per product (advisory lock or SELECT...

240
00:13:37.725 --> 00:13:45.128
FOR UPDATE on a product row), recompute asynchronously from a per-product queue, or use SERIALIZABLE isolation and retry.

241
00:13:46.478 --> 00:13:48.078
Composing the product page.

242
00:13:48.358 --> 00:14:00.198
A product page is assembled from many services: catalog, offers, buy box, price, promotions, delivery promise, reviews, Q and A, recommendations, ads and personalisation.

243
00:14:00.478 --> 00:14:07.369
A 2006 interview described 100 to 150 service calls to build a page (VERIFY:

244
00:14:07.649 --> 00:14:12.022
A Conversation with Werner Vogels, ACM Queue, 2006).

245
00:14:12.302 --> 00:14:15.971
Latency budget: the page deadline is split across tiers.

246
00:14:16.251 --> 00:14:28.113
Calls run in parallel with per-call timeouts, and non-critical widgets degrade: the page renders without recommendations rather than waiting (Ch 18, Ch 56 L1).

247
00:14:29.463 --> 00:14:34.967
Critical versus optional: title, price, buy box, availability and promise are critical.

248
00:14:35.247 --> 00:14:38.639
Everything else is optional and has a fallback or is hidden.

249
00:14:38.919 --> 00:14:41.777
Caching: catalog content is cached at the edge.

250
00:14:42.057 --> 00:14:47.753
Price and availability are fetched fresh, or cached for seconds and re-validated in the cart.

251
00:14:49.103 --> 00:14:50.383
The delivery promise.

252
00:14:50.663 --> 00:15:01.138
Get it by Thursday is computed before an order exists: which FCs hold the item, carrier cut-offs, transit times to the customer's postcode, and FC capacity.

253
00:15:01.418 --> 00:15:06.239
The fulfilment plan chooses FCs per order to meet the promise at minimum cost.

254
00:15:06.519 --> 00:15:09.741
Every extra box costs money and customer goodwill.

255
00:15:10.021 --> 00:15:13.733
L3 finding: planning strategies differ a lot in cost.

256
00:15:14.013 --> 00:15:20.391
Exhaustive planning saves 17% and cuts splits from 54% to 36%.

257
00:15:20.671 --> 00:15:28.053
They are identical on the promise (63.5%), because per-item nearest is already the fastest plan.

258
00:15:29.403 --> 00:15:39.728
What moves the promise is inventory placement: stocking the 300 best-sellers in every FC raised it to 68.7% and cut cost further.

259
00:15:40.008 --> 00:15:51.485
This is why marketplaces invest in forecasting and placement, and in seller-fulfilment programmes that put sellers' stock into the platform's FCs (VERIFY for any specific programme).

260
00:15:51.765 --> 00:15:56.459
Promise integrity: the promise shown on the product page is re-checked at checkout.

261
00:15:56.739 --> 00:16:01.176
The order stores the promise it was sold with, and missed promises are an SLI.

262
00:16:02.526 --> 00:16:03.913
Orders as workflows.

263
00:16:04.193 --> 00:16:19.873
An order is a long-running workflow that can take days: authorise payment, then reserve stock at FCs, then release to FC, then pick, then pack, then ship, then deliver, then capture payment, then settle to sellers, then returns window.

264
00:16:20.153 --> 00:16:24.291
Each step is idempotent and has a compensation (Ch 13).

265
00:16:24.571 --> 00:16:33.211
The workflow's state is persisted, and timers drive timeouts (for example, not shipped in 48 h, then notify, offer cancellation).

266
00:16:33.491 --> 00:16:39.209
Multi-seller orders split into per-seller or per-FC shipments, each with its own sub-workflow.

267
00:16:39.489 --> 00:16:46.657
Payment is captured per shipment when it ships (a common marketplace practice; VERIFY per payment network and country).

268
00:16:48.007 --> 00:16:55.708
Seller settlement: the marketplace collects payment, holds it through the returns window, deducts fees, and pays sellers on a schedule.

269
00:16:55.988 --> 00:17:00.425
That needs a double-entry ledger with per-seller accounts (Ch 45).

270
00:17:01.775 --> 00:17:03.141
Teams and services.

271
00:17:03.421 --> 00:17:12.530
Small, autonomous teams own services end to end, including on-call, often described as two-pizza teams (VERIFY the original sources).

272
00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.711
Services communicate only through interfaces:

273
00:17:15.991 --> 00:17:18.743
APIs and events, no shared databases.

274
00:17:19.023 --> 00:17:26.170
This is widely attributed to an early-2000s internal mandate (VERIFY; the best-known account is second-hand).

275
00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:30.482
The consequence is many services per page (§2.6).

276
00:17:30.762 --> 00:17:42.687
That makes latency budgets, timeouts and degradation first-class concerns, and requires a platform: deployment, observability, service discovery (Ch 16, Ch 37).

277
00:17:44.037 --> 00:17:45.616
Cells and shuffle sharding.

278
00:17:45.896 --> 00:17:51.699
Cells are complete, independent copies of a service stack, each serving a subset of customers.

279
00:17:51.979 --> 00:17:55.008
A bad deploy or a poison request affects one cell.

280
00:17:55.288 --> 00:18:02.733
Deploys roll out cell by cell (Ch 34; VERIFY: re:Invent talks on minimising blast radius).

281
00:18:03.013 --> 00:18:07.899
Shuffle sharding assigns each customer a small random combination of workers.

282
00:18:08.179 --> 00:18:11.144
Two customers rarely share their whole combination.

283
00:18:11.424 --> 00:18:17.504
The chance that another customer shares all k workers with a poison customer is 1 or C(n,k).

284
00:18:18.854 --> 00:18:22.971
For 5 of 80 workers that is 1 in 24 million (L4).

285
00:18:23.251 --> 00:18:28.051
Fixed shards of 5 still take out 6.3% of customers completely.

286
00:18:28.331 --> 00:18:29.206
VERIFY:

287
00:18:29.486 --> 00:18:34.094
AWS Builders' Library, Workload isolation using shuffle-sharding.

288
00:18:34.374 --> 00:18:42.523
The trade-off: with shuffle shards, more customers are degraded, meaning they lose part of their shard (28% for 5 of 80).

289
00:18:42.803 --> 00:18:46.643
They survive only if clients retry on another worker in their shard.

290
00:18:46.923 --> 00:18:52.982
Shuffle sharding therefore requires client-side retries across the shard and per-worker health checks.

291
00:18:54.332 --> 00:19:02.951
Where it fits: multi-tenant front ends, API gateways, queues, and anything where one tenant's traffic can be poison or overwhelming.

292
00:19:04.301 --> 00:19:05.239
Peak events.

293
00:19:05.519 --> 00:19:13.370
Pre-scaling from forecasts, with no reliance on reactive autoscaling alone (Ch 02 §3.3).

294
00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:18.962
Deal inventory: hot deals use reservation gates and queues (Ch 46).

295
00:19:19.242 --> 00:19:27.434
Game days before the event, a freeze on risky changes, and degradation switches ready: hide recommendations, simplify search.

296
00:19:27.714 --> 00:19:33.794
Load shedding by priority: checkout traffic is protected over browse traffic (Ch 18).

297
00:19:35.144 --> 00:19:39.517
Constant-work designs avoid modes that only appear at peak (VERIFY:

298
00:19:39.797 --> 00:19:45.131
AWS Builders' Library, Reliability, constant work, and a good cup of coffee).

299
00:19:46.481 --> 00:19:47.526
Going global.

300
00:19:47.806 --> 00:19:49.171
Here is a quick table:

301
00:19:49.451 --> 00:19:50.774
Concern, and Approach.

302
00:19:51.054 --> 00:19:52.654
Marketplaces per country:

303
00:19:52.934 --> 00:19:58.011
Separate catalogs, prices, tax and payment methods, sharing platform services.

304
00:19:58.291 --> 00:19:59.550
Data residency:

305
00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:07.147
Regional cells holding personal and payment data in-country where required (VERIFY per law) (Ch 36).

306
00:20:07.427 --> 00:20:08.665
Cross-border sellers:

307
00:20:08.945 --> 00:20:14.086
Listing translation, currency conversion, customs and duties, export compliance.

308
00:20:14.366 --> 00:20:15.283
Latency:

309
00:20:15.563 --> 00:20:20.555
Edge caching, regional stacks; global services only for non-personal data.

