432
00:29:05.261 --> 00:29:06.115
Reading it:

433
00:29:06.395 --> 00:29:21.563
Reads: a maintained row serves 57k reads per second at p99 0.29 ms, against 29k per second at p99 0.49 ms when computing the winner per page.

434
00:29:21.843 --> 00:29:23.784
That is about 2 times the throughput.

435
00:29:24.064 --> 00:29:33.152
Writes: maintaining it costs about 30% of repricing throughput (5,058, then 3,570 per second).

436
00:29:33.432 --> 00:29:36.056
The race is real but hard to see under load.

437
00:29:36.336 --> 00:29:43.909
In 5 seconds of a price war on 20 products, the final table was wrong for 1 product in this run (0 in others).

438
00:29:45.259 --> 00:29:50.678
Every later reprice recomputes the winner from committed data, which heals earlier mistakes.

439
00:29:50.958 --> 00:29:54.179
Only a race on the last change leaves a lasting error.

440
00:29:54.459 --> 00:29:57.126
The forced interleaving shows the race directly.

441
00:29:57.406 --> 00:30:04.873
T2 computes its winner from a snapshot that can't see T1's uncommitted price cut, waits on the row, then overwrites it.

442
00:30:05.153 --> 00:30:11.851
The result: seller 1 at 450 rupeesis shown although seller 2 is at 400 rupees.

443
00:30:12.131 --> 00:30:17.443
A per-product advisory lock fixes it, at ~14% of price-war throughput.

444
00:30:17.723 --> 00:30:21.734
Timings vary run to run (about ±15% here).

445
00:30:22.014 --> 00:30:23.550
The race result doesn't.

