Planning doesn't change the promise at all.

470
00:33:05.755 --> 00:33:10.513
All three strategies meet it on the same 63.5% of orders.

471
00:33:10.793 --> 00:33:16.190
Per-item nearest is already the fastest possible plan, and the others only fall back to it.

472
00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:18.049
Placement moves the promise.

473
00:33:18.329 --> 00:33:27.417
Stocking the 300 best-sellers in every FC raised it to 68.7% and cut the cheapest plan to 81.4 rupees.

474
00:33:28.767 --> 00:33:32.777
The rest is long-tail SKUs held only in distant FCs.

475
00:33:33.057 --> 00:33:34.828
No algorithm can fix that.

476
00:33:35.108 --> 00:33:40.932
The exhaustive search costs 35 to 210 µs per order at 1 to 6 items.

477
00:33:41.212 --> 00:33:44.561
Larger baskets need heuristics or integer programming.

478
00:33:45.911 --> 00:33:47.533
L4, Shuffle sharding.

479
00:33:47.813 --> 00:33:48.751
Requirement:

480
00:33:49.031 --> 00:33:51.783
One bad customer must not take down the others.

481
00:33:53.133 --> 00:33:56.440
Here is the Java code, ShuffleShardL4.java.

482
00:33:56.720 --> 00:34:00.411
It runs to 56 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

483
00:34:00.691 --> 00:34:09.565
L4, Shuffle sharding (as described in the AWS Builders' Library): each customer gets a small random subset of. workers.

484
00:34:09.845 --> 00:34:15.456
A poison customer whose requests crash every worker they touch takes out only its own subset.

485
00:34:15.736 --> 00:34:16.632
How many.

486
00:34:16.912 --> 00:34:21.435
OTHER customers lose all of their workers (full outage) or some (degraded)?

487
00:34:21.715 --> 00:34:23.955
Exact combinatorics + simulation.

488
00:34:26.805 --> 00:34:27.722
On screen:

489
00:34:28.002 --> 00:34:45.666
10,000 customers; one poison customer crashes every worker it is routed to. layout workers possible shards full outage degraded. no sharding (everyone on every worker) 8 1 100.000% 0.00%.

490
00:34:45.946 --> 00:35:08.879
4 fixed shards of 2 workers 8 4 24.974% 0.00%. shuffle shards of 2 of 8 workers 8 28 3.558% 42.90%. shuffle shards of 4 of 8 workers 8 70 1.436% 97.12%.

491
00:35:09.159 --> 00:35:32.925
16 fixed shards of 5 workers 80 16 6.274% 0.00%. shuffle shards of 5 of 80 workers 80 24,040,016 0.000% 28.19%. exact chance another customer shares ALL workers with the poison one, shuffle k of n =.

492
00:35:33.205 --> 00:35:34.741
1 or C(n,k):

493
00:35:35.021 --> 00:35:40.503
C(8,2) = 28, then 3.571429%.

494
00:35:40.783 --> 00:35:42.298
The rest is in the chapter.

495
00:35:45.148 --> 00:35:46.001
Reading it:

496
00:35:46.281 --> 00:35:50.441
The simulation matches the exact probability 1 or C(n,k):

497
00:35:50.721 --> 00:35:59.020
3.56% versus 3.57%, and 1.44% versus 1.43%.

498
00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:03.993
Fixed shards of 2 workers: a poison customer takes out a quarter of everyone.

499
00:36:04.273 --> 00:36:05.937
Shuffle shards of 2 of 8:

500
00:36:06.217 --> 00:36:07.647
3.6%.

501
00:36:07.927 --> 00:36:15.948
5 of 80: effectively nobody (1 in 24 million), against 6.3% for fixed shards of the same size.

502
00:36:17.298 --> 00:36:19.367
The cost is in the degraded column.

503
00:36:19.647 --> 00:36:22.186
Many more customers lose part of their shard:

504
00:36:22.466 --> 00:36:26.861
28% for 5 of 80, and 97% for 4 of 8.

505
00:36:27.141 --> 00:36:30.831
They stay up only if clients retry on another worker in their shard.

506
00:36:31.111 --> 00:36:35.335
Shuffle sharding without in-shard retries turns degraded into failed.

507
00:36:36.685 --> 00:36:37.624
Part 6.

508
00:36:37.904 --> 00:36:39.184
Production Concerns.

509
00:36:40.434 --> 00:36:41.586
Failure modes.

510
00:36:41.866 --> 00:36:43.231
Here is a quick table:

511
00:36:43.511 --> 00:36:45.687
Failure, and Effect, and Mitigation.

512
00:36:45.967 --> 00:36:47.482
Cart replica partition.

513
00:36:47.762 --> 00:36:48.551
Effect:

514
00:36:48.831 --> 00:36:49.706
Siblings.

515
00:36:49.986 --> 00:36:50.989
Mitigation:

516
00:36:51.269 --> 00:36:53.423
OR-Set merge (L1), read repair.

517
00:36:53.703 --> 00:36:54.877
Buy-box race.

518
00:36:55.157 --> 00:36:55.946
Effect:

519
00:36:56.226 --> 00:36:57.335
Wrong winner shown.

520
00:36:57.615 --> 00:36:58.618
Mitigation:

521
00:36:58.898 --> 00:37:02.930
Per-product lock (L2), checkout re-validation, nightly check.

522
00:37:03.210 --> 00:37:04.362
Repricer storm.

523
00:37:04.642 --> 00:37:05.431
Effect:

524
00:37:05.711 --> 00:37:07.098
Offer write overload.

525
00:37:07.378 --> 00:37:08.381
Mitigation:

526
00:37:08.661 --> 00:37:11.861
Per-seller rate limits (Ch 39), coalescing.

527
00:37:12.141 --> 00:37:13.207
FC outage.

528
00:37:13.487 --> 00:37:14.277
Effect:

529
00:37:14.557 --> 00:37:15.645
Promises wrong.

530
00:37:15.925 --> 00:37:16.927
Mitigation:

531
00:37:17.207 --> 00:37:20.450
Replan, mark FC unavailable in the promise service.

532
00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:22.415
Poison tenant or request.

533
00:37:22.695 --> 00:37:23.485
Effect:

534
00:37:23.765 --> 00:37:24.789
Workers crash.

535
00:37:25.069 --> 00:37:26.071
Mitigation:

536
00:37:26.351 --> 00:37:29.274
Shuffle sharding (L4), request validation.

537
00:37:29.554 --> 00:37:30.578
Bad deploy.

538
00:37:30.858 --> 00:37:31.647
Effect:

539
00:37:31.927 --> 00:37:32.781
Errors.

540
00:37:33.061 --> 00:37:34.063
Mitigation:

541
00:37:34.343 --> 00:37:36.455
Cell-by-cell rollout with bake time.

542
00:37:37.805 --> 00:37:38.893
Observability.

543
00:37:39.173 --> 00:37:55.728
Useful signals: page p99 per widget;. cart sibling rate, and the share of merges that resurrect items;. buy-box mismatch count from the nightly check;. promise accuracy (delivered by the promised date);. split-shipment rate;. per-cell error rates.

544
00:37:57.078 --> 00:37:58.443
Security and abuse.

545
00:37:58.723 --> 00:38:00.238
Seller account takeover.

546
00:38:00.518 --> 00:38:03.931
Fake reviews: detection, and verified-purchase flags.

547
00:38:04.211 --> 00:38:08.179
Price-gouging controls during emergencies (VERIFY local law).

548
00:38:08.459 --> 00:38:11.937
Bot checkout on deals: queues, CAPTCHAs, per-account limits.

549
00:38:13.287 --> 00:38:14.631
Performance and cost.

550
00:38:14.911 --> 00:38:16.383
Cache catalog at the edge.

551
00:38:16.663 --> 00:38:18.775
Keep price and availability fresh.

552
00:38:19.055 --> 00:38:20.633
Precompute recommendations.

553
00:38:20.913 --> 00:38:24.519
Cost per order is a first-class metric (Ch 35).

554
00:38:24.799 --> 00:38:27.743
Split shipments are a direct cost driver (L3).

555
00:38:29.093 --> 00:38:29.989
Compliance.

556
00:38:30.269 --> 00:38:37.565
Marketplace liability rules, consumer-protection disclosures, tax collection at source for sellers, and data residency.

557
00:38:37.845 --> 00:38:42.303
All of these vary by country (VERIFY per market) (Ch 36).

558
00:38:43.653 --> 00:38:44.592
Part 7.

559
00:38:44.872 --> 00:38:46.579
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

560
00:38:47.829 --> 00:38:48.746
Watch out:

561
00:38:49.026 --> 00:38:49.666
1.

562
00:38:49.946 --> 00:38:51.589
Strongly consistent carts.

563
00:38:51.869 --> 00:38:55.154
An unavailable cart loses sales during partitions.

564
00:38:56.504 --> 00:38:57.421
Watch out:

565
00:38:57.701 --> 00:38:58.405
2.

566
00:38:58.685 --> 00:39:00.136
Last-writer-wins carts.

567
00:39:00.416 --> 00:39:02.720
They silently drop items (L1).

568
00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:04.987
Watch out:

569
00:39:05.267 --> 00:39:06.014
3.

570
00:39:06.294 --> 00:39:08.406
Union-merge without remove tracking.

571
00:39:08.686 --> 00:39:10.883
Deleted items come back (L1).

572
00:39:12.233 --> 00:39:13.151
Watch out:

573
00:39:13.431 --> 00:39:14.135
4.

574
00:39:14.415 --> 00:39:16.996
Computing the buy box on every page at scale.

575
00:39:17.276 --> 00:39:19.836
It wastes database capacity (L2).

576
00:39:21.186 --> 00:39:22.103
Watch out:

577
00:39:22.383 --> 00:39:23.151
5.

578
00:39:23.431 --> 00:39:26.546
Maintaining derived winners without serialization.

579
00:39:26.826 --> 00:39:28.831
A lasting wrong winner (L2).

580
00:39:30.181 --> 00:39:31.099
Watch out:

581
00:39:31.379 --> 00:39:32.125
6.

582
00:39:32.405 --> 00:39:34.283
Trusting cart prices at checkout.

583
00:39:34.563 --> 00:39:36.035
Always re-validate.

584
00:39:37.385 --> 00:39:38.302
Watch out:

585
00:39:38.582 --> 00:39:39.329
7.

586
00:39:39.609 --> 00:39:41.273
Promises from routing alone.

587
00:39:41.553 --> 00:39:43.771
Placement decides the promise (L3).

588
00:39:45.121 --> 00:39:46.039
Watch out:

589
00:39:46.319 --> 00:39:47.001
8.

590
00:39:47.281 --> 00:39:49.031
Ignoring split-shipment cost.

591
00:39:49.311 --> 00:39:51.700
It is a direct cost per order (L3).

592
00:39:53.050 --> 00:39:53.967
Watch out:

593
00:39:54.247 --> 00:39:54.994
9.

594
00:39:55.274 --> 00:39:56.831
Waiting for every widget.

595
00:39:57.111 --> 00:39:58.903
Optional widgets must degrade.

596
00:40:00.253 --> 00:40:01.171
Watch out:

597
00:40:01.451 --> 00:40:02.155
10.

598
00:40:02.435 --> 00:40:04.781
Fixed shards for multi-tenant front ends.

599
00:40:05.061 --> 00:40:07.323
Large blast radius (L4).

600
00:40:08.673 --> 00:40:09.590
Watch out:

601
00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:10.702
11.

602
00:40:10.982 --> 00:40:13.350
Shuffle sharding without client retries.

603
00:40:13.630 --> 00:40:16.894
Degraded customers become failed customers (L4).

604
00:40:18.244 --> 00:40:19.161
Watch out:

605
00:40:19.441 --> 00:40:20.231
12.

606
00:40:20.511 --> 00:40:22.623
Global stacks for regulated data.

607
00:40:22.903 --> 00:40:24.439
Residency violations.

608
00:40:25.789 --> 00:40:26.663
Part 8.

609
00:40:26.943 --> 00:40:28.287
Interview Q and A.

610
00:40:29.537 --> 00:40:30.369
Beginner.

611
00:40:30.649 --> 00:40:31.673
Question 1.

612
00:40:31.953 --> 00:40:35.004
What are the main services of an e-commerce marketplace?

613
00:40:35.284 --> 00:40:36.649
Thirty-second answer:

614
00:40:36.929 --> 00:40:51.244
Catalog, offers or pricing, search, product page aggregation, cart, checkout, inventory, payments, order workflow, fulfilment and shipping, seller portal, reviews, recommendations, each owned by a team with its own data.

615
00:40:52.594 --> 00:40:53.597
Question 2.

616
00:40:53.877 --> 00:40:57.119
Why is a marketplace different from a single-retailer shop?

617
00:40:57.399 --> 00:40:58.765
Thirty-second answer:

618
00:40:59.045 --> 00:41:11.738
Many sellers offer the same product, so the platform must merge listings, choose a winning offer fairly, handle seller inventory and fulfilment methods, and settle money to sellers after fees and returns.

619
00:41:13.088 --> 00:41:14.133
Question 3.

620
00:41:14.413 --> 00:41:16.397
What happens when you click 'Add to cart'?

621
00:41:16.677 --> 00:41:18.043
Thirty-second answer:

622
00:41:18.323 --> 00:41:29.160
The cart service writes the item to replicated storage that accepts writes even if some replicas are down; nothing is reserved yet, price and stock are checked again at checkout.

623
00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:31.577
Question 4.

624
00:41:31.857 --> 00:41:34.246
How would you estimate product-page traffic?

625
00:41:34.526 --> 00:41:35.891
Thirty-second answer:

626
00:41:36.171 --> 00:41:49.462
Daily active users times pages per user ÷ 86,400 for average, times a peak factor (10 times on sale days); multiply by service calls per page for internal load.

627
00:41:50.812 --> 00:41:51.815
Intermediate.

628
00:41:52.095 --> 00:41:53.204
Question 5.

629
00:41:53.484 --> 00:41:55.831
Design the shopping cart for availability.

630
00:41:56.111 --> 00:41:57.476
Thirty-second answer:

631
00:41:57.756 --> 00:42:13.351
A leaderless or multi-leader KV store with sloppy quorums and hinted handoff so writes always succeed; version vectors detect concurrent versions; merge with an OR-Set so adds and removes both survive; re-validate everything at checkout.

632
00:42:14.701 --> 00:42:15.810
Question 6.

633
00:42:16.090 --> 00:42:19.205
How do you choose and serve the winning offer for a product?

634
00:42:19.485 --> 00:42:20.850
Thirty-second answer:

635
00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:34.847
Score offers (landed price, promise, seller performance, stock), maintain the winner per product on every offer change in the same transaction under a per-product lock, serve it from that row, and re-validate at checkout.

636
00:42:36.197 --> 00:42:37.285
Question 7.

637
00:42:37.565 --> 00:42:41.256
How do you keep a product page fast when it needs dozens of services?

638
00:42:41.536 --> 00:42:42.901
Thirty-second answer:

639
00:42:43.181 --> 00:42:56.856
Parallel calls with a page deadline split into per-call timeouts, critical versus optional widgets, fallbacks or hiding for optional ones, edge caching for catalog content, and fresh fetch for price and availability.

640
00:42:58.206 --> 00:42:59.230
Question 8.

641
00:42:59.510 --> 00:43:02.091
How is a delivery date promised before ordering?

642
00:43:02.371 --> 00:43:03.737
Thirty-second answer:

643
00:43:04.017 --> 00:43:15.174
From which FCs hold the item, carrier cut-offs, transit times to the postcode, and FC capacity; cached per product and region, recomputed at checkout, stored on the order.

644
00:43:16.524 --> 00:43:17.591
Question 9.

645
00:43:17.871 --> 00:43:19.151
How are sellers paid?

646
00:43:19.431 --> 00:43:20.796
Thirty-second answer:

647
00:43:21.076 --> 00:43:35.519
The marketplace collects payment, records it in a double-entry ledger with per-seller liability accounts, holds funds through the returns window, deducts fees and refunds, and pays out on a schedule with reconciliation against bank transfers.

648
00:43:36.869 --> 00:43:37.637
Senior.

649
00:43:37.917 --> 00:43:38.962
Question 10.

650
00:43:39.242 --> 00:43:42.378
Two sellers reprice the same product at the same moment.

651
00:43:42.658 --> 00:43:44.877
What can go wrong and how do you prevent it?

652
00:43:45.157 --> 00:43:46.522
Thirty-second answer:

653
00:43:46.802 --> 00:43:57.149
Each transaction recomputes the winner from a snapshot that can't see the other's uncommitted change; the later commit can install a wrong winner that persists until the next change.

654
00:43:57.429 --> 00:44:06.687
Serialize per product (advisory lock or locking a product row), or recompute asynchronously per product, or use SERIALIZABLE with retry.

655
00:44:08.037 --> 00:44:09.168
Question 11.

656
00:44:09.448 --> 00:44:12.669
How do you decide which FCs ship a multi-item order?

657
00:44:12.949 --> 00:44:14.315
Thirty-second answer:

658
00:44:14.595 --> 00:44:28.952
Enumerate plans over FCs holding each item (small in practice), filter by the promise, minimise cost including a per-box fixed cost; fall back to the fastest plan when none meets the promise; re-plan on short picks.

659
00:44:30.302 --> 00:44:31.390
Question 12.

660
00:44:31.670 --> 00:44:36.171
Why does inventory placement matter more than routing for delivery promises?

661
00:44:36.451 --> 00:44:37.817
Thirty-second answer:

662
00:44:38.097 --> 00:44:46.843
Routing can only choose among FCs that already hold the item; if a long-tail item is only far away, no algorithm meets the promise.

663
00:44:47.123 --> 00:44:58.110
Placing best-sellers everywhere raised the promise rate from 63.5% to 68.7% in L3 while routing changes left it unchanged.

664
00:44:59.460 --> 00:45:00.740
Question 13.

665
00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:04.049
How do you run a multi-day order process reliably?

666
00:45:04.329 --> 00:45:05.695
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:45:05.975 --> 00:45:17.900
A persisted workflow with idempotent steps, compensations, durable timers, per-shipment sub-workflows, and visibility into stuck orders, not a chain of synchronous calls or cron jobs.

668
00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:20.445
Question 14.

669
00:45:20.725 --> 00:45:23.306
How do you prepare the platform for a peak sale day?

670
00:45:23.586 --> 00:45:24.951
Thirty-second answer:

671
00:45:25.231 --> 00:45:37.946
Forecast and pre-scale, load test full journeys, gate hot deals with reservation queues, freeze risky changes, prepare degradation switches, protect checkout with priority shedding, and run game days.

672
00:45:39.296 --> 00:45:40.192
Architect.

673
00:45:40.472 --> 00:45:41.667
Question 15.

674
00:45:41.947 --> 00:45:44.464
Explain shuffle sharding and when it's worth it.

675
00:45:44.744 --> 00:45:46.109
Thirty-second answer:

676
00:45:46.389 --> 00:46:05.035
Give each customer a random small combination of k of n workers; a poison customer only kills its own combination, and the chance another customer shares all k is 1 or C(n,k), for 5 of 80, one in 24 million versus 6.3% with fixed shards.

677
00:46:05.315 --> 00:46:10.072
It needs client retries within the shard because more customers are partially affected.

678
00:46:11.422 --> 00:46:12.617
Question 16.

679
00:46:12.897 --> 00:46:15.414
How would you use cells in a global marketplace?

680
00:46:15.694 --> 00:46:17.059
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:46:17.339 --> 00:46:31.185
Partition customers (and sometimes sellers) into independent full-stack cells with a thin routing layer; deploy cell by cell with bake time; keep global services minimal; place regulated data in regional cells.

682
00:46:32.535 --> 00:46:33.879
Question 17.

683
00:46:34.159 --> 00:46:38.063
Where would you accept eventual consistency in this platform, and where not?

684
00:46:38.343 --> 00:46:39.708
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:46:39.988 --> 00:46:45.791
Eventual: catalog, search, reviews, recommendations, cart contents (with correct merges).

686
00:46:46.071 --> 00:46:53.772
Strong: last-unit reservation, payment, ledger, order state, and the price actually charged (re-validated at checkout).

687
00:46:55.122 --> 00:46:56.295
Question 18.

688
00:46:56.575 --> 00:47:00.095
How do you organise hundreds of teams around this architecture?

689
00:47:00.375 --> 00:47:01.741
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:47:02.021 --> 00:47:17.125
Teams own services end to end around business capabilities, communicate through APIs and events only, share a paved-road platform (deploy, observability, discovery), and are held to SLOs and page-level latency budgets.

691
00:47:18.475 --> 00:47:19.712
Question 19.

692
00:47:19.992 --> 00:47:22.552
How would you expand the marketplace to a new country?

693
00:47:22.832 --> 00:47:24.197
Thirty-second answer:

694
00:47:24.477 --> 00:47:38.749
A new marketplace on shared platform services, with regional cells for personal and payment data where required, localised catalog, tax and payments, local FCs and carriers, and phased launch with seller onboarding first.

695
00:47:40.099 --> 00:47:41.209
Question 20.

696
00:47:41.489 --> 00:47:44.859
What would you measure to know the marketplace architecture is healthy?

697
00:47:45.139 --> 00:47:46.505
Thirty-second answer:

698
00:47:46.785 --> 00:48:04.982
Journey SLOs (browse, add to cart, checkout), page p99 by widget, promise accuracy, split-shipment rate and cost per order, buy-box correctness, cart merge anomalies, per-cell error rates and blast radius of incidents, and deploy frequency with change failure rate.

699
00:48:06.332 --> 00:48:07.228
Part 9.

700
00:48:07.508 --> 00:48:08.916
Summary and Practice.

701
00:48:10.166 --> 00:48:11.254
Key takeaways.

702
00:48:11.534 --> 00:48:13.603
Choose consistency per operation.

703
00:48:13.883 --> 00:48:18.001
Carts are always writable; money and the last unit of stock are strong.

704
00:48:18.281 --> 00:48:19.859
Carts need correct merges:

705
00:48:20.139 --> 00:48:23.297
OR-Set, not last-writer-wins or plain union (L1).

706
00:48:23.577 --> 00:48:28.291
Maintain derived winners transactionally and serialize per product (L2).

707
00:48:28.571 --> 00:48:32.027
Routing sets the cost; placement sets the promise (L3).

708
00:48:32.307 --> 00:48:35.251
Cells and shuffle sharding bound the blast radius.

709
00:48:35.531 --> 00:48:39.009
Shuffle sharding needs retries within the shard (L4).

710
00:48:40.359 --> 00:48:41.340
Cheat sheet.

711
00:48:41.620 --> 00:48:42.985
Here is a quick table:

712
